QUIZ.push(
{id:'q83',topic:'t3',src:'practice',q:'Mister Bunny is accused of stealing the jam (slides). What is H0?',o:['Mister Bunny is innocent','Mister Bunny is guilty','The jam has disappeared','There is a 5% chance he is guilty'],a:0,e:'H0 is the initial assumption, the status quo: innocent until proven guilty.'},
{id:'q84',topic:'t3',src:'practice',q:'Anna is not pregnant but the test says “pregnant” (H0: not pregnant). This is…',o:['a type I error (false positive)','a type II error (false negative)','a correct decision','the power of the test'],a:0,e:'Concluding H1 while H0 is true: false positive, probability α.'},
{id:'q85',topic:'t3',src:'practice',q:'Why do we standardize the observed mean (ZSTAT = (x̄ − μ0)/σx̄)?',o:['To read the bounds in a single table (the standardized normal)','To make the sample larger','To remove outliers','Because the mean is always 0'],a:0,e:'No table exists for a normal of mean 30 and dispersion 2: we move the problem to the standardized normal (bounds ±1.96).'},
{id:'q86',topic:'t3',src:'practice',q:'House.SAV: TSTAT = 7.670 with 86 degrees of freedom. Sample size?',o:['87','86','85','7.67'],a:0,e:'df = n − 1 for a one sample t-test.'},
{id:'q87',topic:'t4',src:'practice',q:'Parametric vs non-parametric tests: which statement matches the slides?',o:['Parametric tests rely on assumptions but deliver more usable information; non-parametric tests are backups','Non-parametric tests are always more accurate','Parametric tests need no assumption','Both always give the same p-value'],a:0,e:'Parametric: more accurate (confidence intervals) when assumptions hold; non-parametric: backup when they fail.'},
{id:'q88',topic:'t4',src:'practice',q:'Healthy-Cereals Inc. wants to know if the proportion of contaminated boxes remains the same between two batches. Which test?',o:['χ² test','One sample t-test','Pearson correlation test','Paired t-test'],a:0,e:'At least two samples, a proportion: χ² test (Fisher if expected counts are too small).'},
{id:'q89',topic:'t5',src:'practice',q:'In the SST decomposition of the slides, the distance between the real price of your house and the average price is…',o:['the quantity to explain (part of SST)','the error (SSE)','the explained part (SSR)','the residual'],a:0,e:'A = Yi − Ȳ (to explain) = B (Yi − Ŷi, error) + C (Ŷi − Ȳ, explained).'},
{id:'q90',topic:'t5',src:'practice',q:'r² = 0.58 for house prices explained by surface. Which conclusion is right?',o:['Quality is limited, but this does not mean the surface has no effect','The surface has no effect','58% of predictions are correct','The model is excellent'],a:0,e:'R² measures the share of the variation explained; significance is checked with the t/F tests.'},
{id:'q91',topic:'t6',src:'practice',q:'In the House example, normality of the residuals is rejected because there are too many small residuals. What do the slides conclude?',o:['The model is not rejected: residuals are “better” than expected','The model must be discarded','The coefficients are biased','Only the F test is valid'],a:0,e:'Normality fails in the “good” direction; with a reasonable sample, tests remain usable.'},
{id:'q92',topic:'t6',src:'practice',q:'A residual plot shows long runs of positive then negative residuals over time. Which assumption is violated?',o:['Independence of errors','Linearity','Normality','Equal variance'],a:0,e:'Errors related to each other (seasonal data): independence. Tests and CIs are very sensitive to it.'},
{id:'q93',topic:'t7',src:'practice',q:'What does MSR = SSR/k measure in the F test?',o:['The explained variation per variable','The unexplained variation per degree of freedom','The total variation','The standard error of the estimate'],a:0,e:'F = MSR/MSE: explained variation per variable over unexplained variation per degree of freedom.'},
{id:'q94',topic:'t7',src:'practice',q:'With one explicative variable, the F test and the t test of the slope…',o:['give the same p-value','always disagree','test different H0 that cannot be compared','need two different samples'],a:0,e:'With k = 1, “all β = 0” is the same hypothesis as “β1 = 0”.'},
{id:'q95',topic:'t8',src:'practice',q:'House model (slides): Orientation (South = 1) has p = 0.86, Downtown has 12 534.63 with p = 0.01. Which statement is right?',o:['Compared with a house outside downtown, a downtown house costs about 12 535 € more, everything else the same; orientation is not interpreted','South-facing houses cost 342.54 € more','Both variables are significant','Downtown explains 12% of the price'],a:0,e:'Interpret only significant dummies, against the reference group.'},
{id:'q96',topic:'t8',src:'practice',q:'Car colour: blue, white, red. How many dummy variables?',o:['2','3','1','0: use codes 1, 2, 3'],a:0,e:'m − 1 = 2 dummies (Blue, White); red is the reference group.'},
{id:'q97',topic:'t8',src:'practice',q:'A sub-model explaining surface by the other X has R² = 0.8. VIF of surface?',o:['5','0.8','1.25','8'],a:0,e:'1/(1 − 0.8) = 5: the threshold of multicollinearity.'},
{id:'q98',topic:'t9',src:'practice',q:'Why does Kaiser’s rule keep components with an eigenvalue above 1?',o:['Because the eigenvalues sum to the number of variables: above 1, a component explains more than one variable on average','Because loadings must be above 1','Because communalities must be above 1','Because the % of variance must be above 1%'],a:0,e:'With p standardized variables, total variance = p; equal sharing would give 1 each.'},
{id:'q99',topic:'t9',src:'practice',q:'FruitCo (slides): PC1 has eigenvalue 5.562 among 10 variables. Its % of variance?',o:['55.62%','5.562%','10%','62%'],a:0,e:'5.562/10 = 55.62%.'},
{id:'q100',topic:'t9',src:'practice',q:'A loading of −0.82 on component 2 means…',o:['the variable is strongly related to component 2, in the opposite direction of the positive loadings','the variable is not related to component 2','the variable must be removed','the communality is negative'],a:0,e:'|loading| &gt; 0.6: related; the sign shows the direction (opposition with positive loadings).'}
);
CARDS.push(
{id:'c38',topic:'t3',f:'H0 / H1 (slides’ analogies)',b:'H0: status quo (innocent, not pregnant, no effect)\nH1: the claim; never contains =, ≥, ≤'},
{id:'c39',topic:'t3',f:'Type I vs type II error',b:'Type I: reject a true H0 (false positive, α)\nType II: keep a false H0 (false negative, β)'},
{id:'c40',topic:'t3',f:'Standardization (ZSTAT)',b:'(x̄ − μ0)/(σ/√n); compare with ±1.96 (5%, two-tailed)\nσ unknown → Student t with n − 1 df'},
{id:'c41',topic:'t3',f:'p-value (3 definitions)',b:'geometric: area further than the observed value\nstatistical: P(equal or more extreme | H0 true)\npractical: reject H0 if p < α'},
{id:'c42',topic:'t4',f:'Parametric vs non-parametric',b:'Parametric: assumptions, more information (CI)\nNon-parametric: backup when assumptions fail'},
{id:'c43',topic:'t5',f:'SST = SSR + SSE (picture)',b:'Yi − Ȳ (to explain) = (Yi − Ŷi) error + (Ŷi − Ȳ) explained'},
{id:'c44',topic:'t6',f:'Three residual-plot problems',b:'curve → non-linearity\ncone → heteroscedasticity\nruns above/below 0 → independence'},
{id:'c45',topic:'t7',f:'MSR and MSE',b:'MSR = SSR/k: explained per variable\nMSE = SSE/(n − k − 1): unexplained per df\nF = MSR/MSE; √MSE = SYX'},
{id:'c46',topic:'t8',f:'Dummy coefficient (interpretation)',b:'“Compared with [reference], [category] is b units higher/lower, everything else remaining the same” (if significant)'},
{id:'c47',topic:'t9',f:'PCA: geometric idea',b:'PC1 along the largest dispersion, PC2 orthogonal…\nkeep the few components that keep most information'},
{id:'c48',topic:'t9',f:'% of variance of a component',b:'eigenvalue / number of variables\n(the eigenvalues sum to the number of variables)'}
);
